1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe acute angle between $x$ and $S$ is the acute angle between $x$ and ${\\text{proj}}_{S}x.$\n\nComputing the cosine using the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1)}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{\\sqrt{8}\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{\\pi }{6},\n$$\n\nwhich is already an acute angle." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186416360 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186416364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416360 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "22bcf45c-640c-575f-a16d-c7b5c8b62a7e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416360 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186416358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416361 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186416363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416361 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a574829-aeac-59dc-b7f2-f0730e2342d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416361 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186184778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416362 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186416361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416362 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a7cb447b-1a24-58bb-a8de-9b45b9c2e4bb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416362 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186416359 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416363 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186416360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186416363 #ed